The goroutine closures capture the loop variable `i` by reference. In Go 1.21, the `for` loop uses a single `i` variable that is reused every iteration, so by the time the goroutines run, `i` is already `5`.

Fix it by passing the current value of `i` into each goroutine:

```go
go func(i int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)
}(i)
```

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(i int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(i)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

