The line

```rust
result *= i;
```

overflows a `u64`. `u64::MAX` is `18446744073709551615`, and `21!` is already larger than that. `25!` is `15511210043330985984000000`, so it cannot fit in a `u64` at all.

In Rust release builds, integer overflow checks are off by default, so unsigned overflow wraps modulo `2^64` silently. That is why `factorial(25)` returns a wrong value without crashing.

One caveat: with the code exactly as shown, a default Rust debug build would not “work perfectly” for `factorial(25)` either — it would panic with an arithmetic overflow error at `i = 21`, because `25!` does not fit in `u64`. If it appeared to work in debug, you likely tested only `factorial(20)`, disabled overflow checks, or used a non-default profile.

To make `factorial(25)` produce the correct value, use a wider integer type such as `u128`. Using `checked_mul` also avoids future silent overflow for larger inputs.

<solution language="rust" verdict="fix">
fn factorial(n: u64) -> u128 {
    let mut result: u128 = 1;
    for i in 1..=n {
        result = result.checked_mul(i as u128).expect("factorial overflow");
    }
    result
}

fn main() {
    println!("{}", factorial(20));
    println!("{}", factorial(25));
}
</solution>

